#PROJECT
PR:PROJECT_TITLE                 	Glutathione-deficiency promotes basal hyperinsulinemia in the insulin secreting
PR:PROJECT_TITLE                 	cell line INS-1 (832/13)
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	This study sought to determine the impact of Glutathione (GSH) synthesis on
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	β-cell function. Glutamate-cysteine ligase catalytic subunit (Gclc) is
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	essential for GSH synthesis. To induce GSH deficiency, GCLC was inhibited in
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	INS-1 (832/13) cells using buthionine sulfoximine (BSO). The 24-hour treatment
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	of INS-1 cells with BSO (250 µM) reduced GSH levels to 33% of untreated,
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	however, glucose-stimulated insulin secretion was unchanged. The 24-hour
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	treatment of INS-1 cells with BME-free medium containing BSO further reduced GSH
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	levels to 9% of untreated and elicited significantly higher basal insulin
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	secretion. We sought to determine if higher basal insulin secretion was related
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	to metabolic alteration therefore, we performed a metabolomics screen. This data
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	is presented here. Out of 53 identified metabolites, we found 6 with
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	significantly lower abundance in -BME+BSO as compared to -BME. Those include,
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	adenosine monophosphate, deoxycytidine monophosphate, glucose 1-phosphate,
PR:PROJECT_SUMMARY               	ADP-glucose, thymidine monophostphase, and deoxycytidine diphosphate.
PR:INSTITUTE                     	Yale University
PR:LAST_NAME                     	Davidson
